course: politics
lesson: 27
title: Israel and Gaza at school and online
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A war far away can arrive in your classroom: some classmates have family in Israel, some in Gaza, and many feel strongly. A flag on a bag is usually a feeling for people far away, not a threat to you.
explain: News reports said hate against Jewish people and hate against Muslims both rose sharply in many countries after 7 October. Blaming a Jewish classmate for what Israel's government does, or calling a Muslim girl a «terrorist», is hate, every time.
explain: Online, many «war» clips were fake: old videos from other wars, things made by computer, or even scenes from video games. Ask when, where and who filmed it before you share.
explain: You do not have to pick a team. Like a friend who is sad for two cousins at once, your heart can hold the hostages and the children of Gaza together.
explain: Words from the scenes: «bruv» (London) = mate, «innit» = isn't it; «it's giving» = it looks like; «piss off» = go away (rude); «howay man» (North East England) = come on; «delulu» = believing something unreal; «greetin'» (Scotland) = crying.
fact: Many children wear a flag to show care for people far away, and schools ask pupils who disagree to talk, not shout.
fact: News reports said antisemitism, hate against Jewish people, rose sharply in many countries after 7 October, and many synagogues and Jewish schools added guards.
fact: News reports said Islamophobia, hate against Muslims, also rose sharply after 7 October.
fact: Blaming a classmate for what a government far away does is unfair and a kind of hate.
fact: People argue about where fair criticism of Israel's government ends and antisemitism begins.
fact: «From the river to the sea» means the land from the Jordan River to the Mediterranean; many Palestinians and supporters say it means freedom and equal rights, while many Jewish people hear it as a call for Israel to disappear.
fact: During the war many clips online were fake, made by computer, taken from video games, or old videos from other wars such as Syria's, fact-checkers found.
fact: In many countries people marched for a ceasefire and help for Gaza, and others gathered for the hostages; most marches were peaceful, and police sometimes arrested people for hateful signs or fighting.
fact: You can feel for Israeli and Palestinian families at the same time.
fact: In Israel most adults serve in the army, and many are called back as reservists in wartime.
fact: A boycott means refusing to buy something as a protest; during the war some shops were boycotted over real links and some over false rumours.
exercise: Why might one boy wear a Palestinian flag badge and another an Israeli flag on his bag? | To show which people they feel for | To show they hate each other | Because it is school uniform | To win a school prize
exercise: What should the two boys do when they disagree? | Talk, not shout, and ask what the flag means to each other | Fight until one gives in | Take each other's flags away and hide them in the lost property box | Make everyone in class choose a team
exercise: A boy asks Ben, born in Chicago, «Why does your country bomb kids?» What is wrong with this? | It blames a Jewish kid for a government far away, which is a kind of hate | Ben is in charge of Israel's army | Nothing, it is a fair question to ask Ben, because everyone born there or nearby must answer for it | Ben has bombed somebody himself
exercise: What is antisemitism? | Hate or prejudice against Jewish people | A kind of medicine | A kind of flag | A love of history
exercise: What is Islamophobia? | Hate or prejudice against Muslims | A fear of islands | A kind of map | A religious holiday
exercise: Amira is called a «terrorist» on the bus. What should she do? | Tell a trusted adult, because it is hate and not her fault | Keep it secret forever, so that nobody else gets into any trouble | Agree with the boy, so that he leaves her alone the next time | Stop going to school and stay at home until the boy forgets her
exercise: Which river and sea does the chant «from the river to the sea» mean? | The Jordan River and the Mediterranean Sea | The Thames and the North Sea | The Nile and the Red Sea | The Amazon and the Atlantic
exercise: Why does the chant scare some people and inspire others? | Some hear freedom and equal rights; others hear a call for Israel to disappear | Everyone agrees it means the same thing, so it cannot scare anyone who hears it | It is a song from a cartoon | Nobody has ever heard it
exercise: Tyler's «Gaza tonight» clip was from a video game. What should you ask before sharing a war video? | When, where and who filmed it? | How many likes does it have? | Is the music good? | Does it look exciting?
exercise: What did fact-checkers find many fake war clips were? | Old videos from other wars, or scenes from video games | Live pictures sent back from the Moon by the astronauts | Cartoons drawn by young children for a school competition | Old weather reports that were filmed on very stormy nights
exercise: Mia sees a big ceasefire march and a smaller crowd for the hostages. What is true in a free country? | You can march, and so can people who disagree with you | Only one side may ever march, and the other must stay at home | Marches are banned everywhere, so both crowds will be arrested | Counter-protests are always violent and should be stopped
exercise: Oisin is sad for the hostages and for the children in Gaza. Is that allowed? | Yes, you can feel for both peoples at once | No, you must pick one side | Only grown-ups may feel sad | Only if your teacher says so
exercise: Lina's grandmother's house in Gaza was destroyed. What is a good thing to say? | «I'm sorry, I'm thinking of your family» | «That's just the news, get over it, it's not real» | «Whose side are you on, then? Tell me first» | Nothing at all; just ignore her and walk past
exercise: Why was Yael's uncle called up to the army? | In Israel most adults serve, and many are called back as reservists | He wanted a holiday | He was picked for a TV show about soldiers that films ordinary people in their first year in the army | He was a famous footballer
exercise: Another kid says of Yael's uncle, «Good, he deserves it.» Why is that wrong? | Wishing harm on a friend's family is cruel | Her uncle is a cartoon character, not a real man | Yael said it first, so the kid was only copying | Uncles cannot be soldiers, so the kid was confused
exercise: What is a boycott? | Refusing to buy something as a protest | A kind of boat | A team sport | A shop sale
test: A boy in London says: «bruv, that kid's got an Israeli flag on his bag, so he hates Palestinians, innit.» What is fairer? | A flag usually shows who someone feels for, not who they hate, so ask him | Everyone with a flag hates the other side, so you should keep away from anyone carrying one | Flags are only ever for football | He must be a soldier
test: An older boy says Amira's headscarf is «giving terrorist». What is true? | It is a hateful lie; most Muslims, like most people, just want to live safely | A headscarf is a sign of danger | It is just a harmless joke, because the older boy did not really mean it and everyone laughed | Amira is to blame for being called it
test: A kid shouts at Ben: «your lot bomb kids, piss off!» What should a friend nearby do? | Stand with Ben and tell a teacher, because blaming him is hate | Laugh along so you fit in | Ask Ben to apologise for Israel in front of everyone, so the shouting stops | Say nothing, because it's not your problem
test: What would most likely happen if Tyler shared the video game clip as real news? | Many people would believe a fake and get angry about something that didn't happen | It would become true | Nothing, because fake videos do no harm as long as people only watch them and do not share them again | The game company would stop the war
test: A kid in Newcastle tells Oisin: «howay man, just pick a side like everyone else.» What is the best reply? | I can care about the hostages and Gaza's children at the same time | If I don't pick, I'm not allowed to talk about it at school or anywhere else at all | Picking a side would stop the war | Only one side has children
test: Chloe in Montreal asks: «one friend says the chant means freedom, the other says it's scary — who's delulu?» What is the fair answer? | Neither; it means different things to different people, so ask what they mean | The first friend, because chants mean nothing at all and nobody could be scared by one | The second friend, because chants are only songs | Both, because rivers can't be in a chant
test: A post says «Don't buy from this burger shop, it supports Israel.» What should Jake do first? | Check whether the claim is true before passing it on | Share it straight away, before the shop has time to deny it | Shout at the shop's workers until they all say sorry | Buy twice as many burgers to show that the post is wrong
test: A girl in Glasgow says: «Lina's greetin' her eyes out.» What would comfort Lina? | Simple kindness, like «I'm sorry, I'm thinking of your family» | Asking her about death numbers and who is to blame for them, so she can talk | Telling her to stop crying | Starting an argument about blame
test: In a cartoon the hero says everyone from one country is evil. What is the real-world lesson? | Never blame a whole people, or a classmate, for what a government does | Cartoons are always right about countries, because grown-ups check them before they are shown | Whole peoples share one brain | Only governments have feelings
test: Which sentence about the marches is fair? | Most were peaceful, and police sometimes arrested people for hateful signs or fighting | Every march was violent | Nobody marched anywhere | Marches are illegal in New Zealand, so the police stopped every one of them and arrested everybody there
test: Where exactly do people disagree about criticising Israel? | Where fair criticism of its government ends and antisemitism begins | Whether Jewish people exist | Whether Israel is in South America | Whether criticism of any government is allowed anywhere in the world at all, even in a free country
exam: Identify the term denoting hatred of or prejudice against Jewish people. | Antisemitism | Islamophobia | Secularism | Solidarity
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attribution of a foreign government's conduct to a classmate on account of his religion? | It is an unjust and hateful act, irrespective of one's view of that government | It is a legitimate form of political debate | It is required by the rules of most schools | It is permissible provided the classmate was born abroad or holds the citizenship of that state
exam: Select the response consistent with the established findings of fact-checkers concerning video material circulated online during the war. | Many clips were fabricated, taken from video games, or recorded in earlier conflicts | All clips were verified as authentic prior to circulation by the platforms on which they appeared | No video material whatsoever was circulated | All clips originated from the United Nations
exam: State the geographical features referred to in the slogan «from the river to the sea». | The Jordan River and the Mediterranean Sea | The River Nile and the Red Sea | The River Thames and the North Sea | The River Danube and the Black Sea
